.TH EUCLID-CLI-ESM-DELETE-BUCKET 1 "August 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.53" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-esm-delete-bucket \- delete a storage bucket
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli esm delete-bucket
.BR \-\-ern | \-e
.I ERN
[\fB\-\-async\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Deletes a storage bucket identified by its Euclid resource name (ERN),
along with every object it contains.
.PP
The objects go with the bucket because nothing else could ever remove
them: an object is only reachable through its bucket, so a row left
behind would be unreachable for good, and the file it names would be
disk nothing accounts for. Each object's stored file is removed and one
.I esm.object.deleted
event is published per object, exactly as if each had been deleted on
its own \- so anything listening for objects in the bucket learns which
of them went, rather than only that the bucket is gone.
.PP
To empty a bucket while keeping it \- optionally only under a key prefix
\- use
.BR euclid-cli-esm-purge-bucket (1)
instead.
.PP
This cannot be undone.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-e ", " \-\-ern " " \fIERN\fR
Euclid resource name of the bucket to delete. Required.
.TP
.B \-\-async
Answer at once and remove the objects in the background, for a bucket
large enough that emptying it takes minutes.
.PP
Without it the whole removal happens inside the request: with a million
objects that is a million round trips, far longer than the gateway's
backend timeout, so the call fails with a timeout while the removal
carries on unseen behind the abandoned request. With it the answer is
.I "HTTP 202"
carrying the object count at the time of asking, and a thread inside ESM
does the work.
.PP
The bucket itself is deleted
.I last,
once its objects are gone, so it stays listed \- and still deletable \-
until it genuinely is. That is what makes the operation resumable: if
ESM is stopped or scaled down halfway through, asking again picks up
where it left off, rather than leaving objects behind that nothing could
reach. Watch it drain with
.BR euclid-cli-esm-list-objects (1).
.PP
The bucket may be given by
.B name
instead of by ERN, in which case it is resolved in your session's account
and namespace; see
.B NAMES AND ERNS
in
.BR euclid-cli-esm (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The bucket was deleted. No output is printed.
.TP
.B 1
Missing/invalid arguments, the server could not be reached, or no
bucket with that ERN exists.
.SH EXAMPLES
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli --endpoint http://localhost:5566 esm delete-bucket \e
    --ern ern:esm:eu-central-1:000000000000:bucket:reports
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli-esm (1),
.BR euclid-cli-esm-create-bucket (1),
.BR euclid-cli-esm-purge-bucket (1),
.BR euclid-cli-esm-list-objects (1)
